{# Phase 1b — Explain block LLM prompt.

   Variables:
     opening_name        — e.g. "Italian Game"
     eco                 — ECO code, e.g. "C50"
     line_id             — slug, e.g. "ruy-lopez-closed-main"
     plies               — list of { index: int, san: str, fen_after: str }
     gold_blocks_json    — pre-truncated few-shot blocks from the gold sidecar
                           (JSON string)

   The template renders a single message body. Consumer wraps it in the
   Anthropic SDK's `messages.create({...})` call with `cache_control` on the
   few-shot block to reduce token spend across multi-line batches.
#}
You are an opening-theory annotator. Given a chess opening line, produce
per-ply rationale for a beginner audience.

Output STRICT JSON conforming to this shape:

{
  "line_id": "<string>",
  "schema_version": 2,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "rationale": "<1-3 sentences, beginner-friendly>",
      "arrows": [{"from": "<sq>", "to": "<sq>", "color": "green|red|blue"}],
      "highlights": [{"square": "<sq>", "intent": "focus|threat|support"}],
      "threats": "<optional 'if X then Y' note, else null>",
      "pauseMs": <optional int 500-20000, else null>
    }
  ]
}

Rules:
  - Exactly ONE block per ply, in order.
  - rationale: 1-3 sentences. Name pieces by descriptive role
    ("the king's bishop"), not just SAN. Avoid engine eval scores.
  - arrows: at most 2 per ply.
    - green = the move that is about to play or a planned follow-up
    - red   = a threat being prevented or piece under attack
    - blue  = piece coordination (battery, support)
  - highlights: at most 3 squares.
    - focus   = the square the move fights for
    - threat  = a square the opponent could exploit
    - support = a square we shore up
  - threats: optional. Only set on moves with a real tactical alarm.
  - pauseMs: leave null for normal moves. Bump to 3500-4000 for critical
    turning-point moves the learner needs extra time to absorb.

Few-shot example (gold reference, abbreviated):

```json
{{ gold_blocks_json }}
```

Now annotate the following line.

Opening: {{ opening_name }} ({{ eco }})
Line: {{ line_id }}

Moves (SAN with resulting FEN):

{% for ply in plies -%}
  {{ ply.index }}. {{ ply.san }}    FEN after: {{ ply.fen_after }}
{% endfor %}

Produce the JSON object now. Do not include any prose, markdown fences, or
commentary outside the JSON.
